digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper proposes to determine whether direct mechanical coupling of cell membranes through red-light–activated artificial adhesions is sufficient, without canonical cadherin-cytoskeleton linkage, to raise membrane tension, activate PLD2-generated phosphatidic acid and downstream mTOR signaling, and transform individually migrating cells into a faster, coordinated collective, thereby establishing membrane-based cues as drivers of collective cell migration.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) The retained and repaired terminal conclusions jointly motivate the paper-level research proposal."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) To explore membrane-based signaling in collective cell dynamics, the study used artificial photoswitchable Cph1-based cell-cell adhesions designed to selectively elicit signals originating within the plasma membrane."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Changes in membrane properties can both arise from mechano-chemical feedback, intracellular signaling, or gene-expression changes and influence leading-edge dynamics and migration behavior in single cells."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) The need for tools that isolate plasma-membrane mechanical cues cannot be met simply by using E-cadherin constructs lacking cytoplasmic catenin-binding domains, because such truncation renders E-cadherin nonfunctional for sustaining intercellular adhesions."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Increasing cell-cell connections in cells that otherwise migrate individually is associated with coordinated collective movement and, in the reported condition, faster rather than slower migration."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) In the photoactivated cell-cell adhesion condition, increased adhesion produced both a rise in membrane tension and activation of the PLD2 pathway, increasing phosphatidic acid and activating mTOR signaling."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Classical cadherins have dual roles in cell-cell adhesion: they mechanically connect neighboring cells through extracellular and cytoplasmic-catenin linkages and also activate signaling circuits that regulate cytoskeletal dynamics and gene expression."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Protein-independent signaling cascades originating within the plasma membrane are plausible mediators of sustained cell-dynamic changes, because plasma-membrane changes can have long-term effects."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Whether plasma membrane-derived signaling cues contribute to collective cell migration independently of adhesion-cytoskeleton linkage remains an overlooked and unresolved question."];
  "c1" [label="(0,0,0) induction: Given the case of collective cell migration is a hallmark of morphogenesis, tissue homeostasis, and wound healing, and the common that the strength of intercellular adhesions determines whether cells migrate collectively or individually, therefore adhesion strength is a central element in collective cell migration."];
  "c3" [label="(0,0,0) abduction: Given the phenomenon of recent studies challenge the view, and the knowledge that the membrane is often regarded as a passive structure enabling only transmembrane protein complex signaling, therefore the hypothesis is that membrane properties actively influence cell dynamics beyond passive separation."];
  "s1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently collective cell migration is a hallmark of morphogenesis, tissue homeostasis, and wound healing."];
  "s10" [label="(10,0,0) Currently changes at the plasma membrane not only yield short-term changes in cell, but also have long-term effects."];
  "s11" [label="(11,0,0) Currently protein-independent signaling cascades that originate from within the plasma membrane are suitable for initiating sustained changes in cell dynamics."];
  "s12" [label="(12,0,0) Currently although it is evident that membrane mechanics regulate the dynamics of individual cells, the contribution of plasma membrane-derived signals to collective cell migration—independent of the direct link between adhesions and cytoskeleton and the associated biochemical signaling cascades—remains unclear."];
  "s13" [label="(13,0,0) Currently a significant challenge in addressing this question is the lack of tools capable of dissecting the mechanical cues at the plasma membrane from the mechano-transduction through cell-cell adhesion molecules to the cytoskeleton and biochemical signals."];
  "s14" [label="(14,0,0) Currently the truncation of the cytoplasmic catenin-binding domains renders E-cadherin nonfunctional, as the membrane-anchored extracellular domain alone is insufficient to sustain intercellular cell-cell adhesions."];
  "s15" [label="(15,0,0) Currently we set out to explore the relevance of membrane-based signaling for collective cell dynamics."];
  "s16" [label="(16,0,0) Currently to selectively elicit signals that originate from within the plasma membrane, we used artificial photoswitchable cell-cell adhesions based on the cyanobacterial phytochrome 1 (Cph1), which induce cell-cell adhesions on red light illumination without directly linking to the actin cytoskeleton."];
  "s17" [label="(17,0,0) Currently an increase in cell-cell connections leads to coordinated and collective cell movement in cells that otherwise would migrate as single cells."];
  "s18" [label="(18,0,0) Currently increased cell-cell adhesions did not slow cells down, as would be expected when augmenting friction, but made them faster."];
  "s19" [label="(19,0,0) Currently the photoactivated increase in cell-cell adhesions resulted in an increase in membrane tension at the cellular level."];
  "s2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently the strength of intercellular adhesions determines whether cells migrate collectively or individually."];
  "s20" [label="(20,0,0) Currently the photoactivated increase in cell-cell adhesions also activated the enzyme phospholipase D2 (PLD2), resulting in an increase in phosphatidic acid (PA) and activation of mammalian target of rapamycin (mTOR) signaling."];
  "s3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently classical cadherins mediate cell-cell adhesions by linking neighboring cells through interactions between their extracellular domains, while α-catenin and β-catenin recruit to the cytoplasmic tail, forming a mechanosensitive link to the actin cytoskeleton."];
  "s4" [label="(4,0,0) If cadherins mechanically connect cells as structural elements and activate intercellular signaling circuits that regulate cytoskeletal dynamics and gene expression, then cadherins have dual mechanical and signaling roles."];
  "s5" [label="(5,0,0) Currently the contribution of signaling cues originating directly from the plasma membrane in collective cell dynamics has been largely overlooked."];
  "s6" [label="(6,0,0) Currently the membrane is often regarded as a passive structure whose primary role is to separate the cell’s exterior from its interior, thereby enabling signal transduction via transmembrane protein complexes."];
  "s7" [label="(7,0,0) Currently recent studies challenge this view."];
  "s8" [label="(8,0,0) Currently changes in membrane properties have been shown to significantly influence leading edge dynamics and migration behavior in single cells."];
  "s9" [label="(9,0,0) Currently alterations in membrane properties are accomplished not only by direct mechano-chemical feedback loops, but also by intracellular signaling and changes in gene expression."];
